// title: Ranking the Transcendentals
// date: 2026-10-12
// description:

#import "../template.typ": *
#show: post

One day in college I went on a walk with a chaplain and a group of students who hardly knew one another, so the chaplain suggested an icebreaker.

He asked us to rank the three transcendentals from greatest to least.

In medieval scholastic philosophy, the transcendentals are the properties that belong to everything that exists by nature of its existence. Commonly, they are Truth, Goodness, and Beauty.

- Truth is what is really there. With enough effort, it is what all logical and independent investigations of reality would eventually converge towards.#footnote[Not every philosopher would agree.]
- Goodness is what you are pleased to have, or what is desirable.
- Beauty is what you are pleased simply to look at, or what is delightful to behold.

The idea is that these three attributes "transcend" Aristotle's categories, and they apply to every being. So in this sense, the question my chaplain posed is nonsense; if everything, at its most fundamental, is at once True, Good, and Beautiful, what would it mean to rank them?

But of course, we don't always see things on such a metaphysical scale. In human terms there are plenty of _beautiful_ theories that are just not _true_. Or _true_ things that are not _good_. So, after spending a moment thinking about the question, we all came up with our personal answers. And I loved this question, because each individual person had their own completely different take on why they picked their specific ranking out of all six possible permutations.

I think "icebreaker" turned out to be the wrong word for this question. If you've ever seen footage of an iceberg turning over in the Arctic---the way, once the waves settle, it shows a deep luminous blue that was underneath the whole time---it was more like that. An iceberg-flipper: a question that turned a person over and revealed a depth that had been there all along.

And, after considering for a while myself, I decided that beauty > goodness > truth.

I say this because this is the order in which I connect with God. Underneath everything, God set our hearts to be oriented to receive truth. The heart is made for, and demands, truth, because all truth is ultimately found in God. How often do we sense a deep unsettlement when, beneath all our reasoning and excuses, we know we are not living for the truth?

So goodness, at least as I experience it, is the gift that takes us there. In the joy of talking with a close friend, or the love of a family member, we receive not only the gift itself but also a drawing closer to God.

Beauty is the moment that we see the gift and the demand belong together. That is, when we recognize that the good gift has filled the heart's demand for truth. Sometimes, it arrives as a glimpse that wakes the demand before any gift has answered it. Sometimes it comes later, once the gift has arrived, and we see that it fits. Monsignor Luigi Giussani,#footnote[The chaplain of this story was part of the CL movement and encouraged me to read Giussani, which I did with great difficulty.] who has shaped much of my thinking here, calls this experience _correspondence_.

Ultimately, I rank them this way because while my heart demands truth, I first recognize it as beauty, through the experience of correspondence: when something good meets me and I find it matches what I was made for.

I have had a hard time reconciling that, objectively, truth is the end that goodness and beauty serve, whereas I rank them the other way because that matches the felt order of correspondence. In some sense, I am Kierkegaard's aesthete, i.e., I care more for the glimpse of beauty than the actual goodness or truth fitting. This hurts me because it makes it difficult to stick with my faith when I don't find it beautiful. I can satisfy and distract myself by collecting glimpses of apparent beauty and never completing correspondence.

He's not here to tell me, but Kierkegaard would say the way out of the aesthetic life is to reverse the order: truth > goodness > beauty.

Earlier, I said that truth is what all independent investigations of reality would converge upon. The problem is that for the deepest truths, the heart cannot wait for convergence. We can never fully rationally justify some of the deepest aspects of our faith. So, if truth comes last, I will wait forever for beauty to carry me to it. The only way to have the deepest truth is to hold it before it has been verified.

I think this is what he meant when he said that truth is subjectivity: the deepest truth is something objectively uncertain that must be held with the most passionate inwardness before we can verify that it is beautiful or good. This is what he calls faith. It is a leap that requires committing to the truth before any correspondence.

I don't think faith's ordering of the transcendentals abandons beauty. It just moves it from the beginning of a life to its end. Concretely, that means committing to prayer when the liturgy is not beautiful, trusting that it is. Or committing to a person, or a vocation, before you have seen that it fits.
